---
import Site from "../layouts/Site.astro";
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL;
const pattern = [
  0, 0, 1, 1, 0, 0, 0, 1, 2, 2, 1, 0, 1, 2, 3, 3, 2, 1, 1, 2, 3, 3, 2, 1, 0, 1,
  2, 2, 1, 0, 0, 0, 1, 1, 0, 0,
];
---

<Site>
  <section class="hero wrap">
    <div>
      <p class="eyebrow">
        <span class="live-dot"></span> ELECTRONICS · COMPUTING · COMMUNICATIONS
      </p>
      <h1>
        Small lights.
        <br />
        <em>Big ideas.</em>
      </h1>
      <p class="intro">
        Learn electronics, computing and communications by making things with
        microcontrollers: small computers that connect ideas to the physical
        world.
      </p>
      <p>
        Make a light glow. Find out how power, instructions and messages make it
        happen. Use that understanding to imagine what else you could build.
        KajLights is an experimental workshop for curious people, with a browser
        playground and projects to make on your desk.
      </p>
      <div class="actions">
        <a class="button dark" href="https://kajlights.github.io/lightvm/">
          Try without a board <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span>
        </a>
        <a class="text-link" href={`${base}build/`}>
          Build your first lights →
        </a>
      </div>
    </div>
    <figure class="light-study">
      <div class="study-label">
        <span>01 / A LITTLE SYSTEM</span>
        <span>INPUT → RULE → LIGHT</span>
      </div>
      <div
        class="led-grid"
        role="img"
        aria-label="A repeating light pattern on a six by six LED grid"
      >
        {pattern.map((n, i) => (
          <span class={`led level-${n}`} style={`--i:${i}`}></span>
        ))}
      </div>
      <figcaption>
        A simple rule can make a surprising pattern.
        <br />
        The interesting part is finding out why.
      </figcaption>
    </figure>
  </section>
  <aside class="wrap" aria-labelledby="early-testing">
    <div class="testing-note">
      <p class="eyebrow">A WORKSHOP IN PROGRESS</p>
      <h2 id="early-testing">Early testing. A few rough edges included.</h2>
      <p>
        KajLights is still a proof of concept. Expect bugs, missing features and
        gaps in the instructions — sometimes all three at once. Things will
        change, and a board may need wiping and setting up again. This is a
        place to experiment, rather than a finished kit or a dependable
        home-lighting system just yet.
      </p>
      <p>
        Something confusing or broken?{" "}
        <a href="https://github.com/KajLights/KajLights.github.io/issues">
          Tell us what you tried
        </a>
        . Finding the rough edges helps make the next person’s first steps
        easier.
      </p>
    </div>
  </aside>
  <section class="wrap section split">
    <div>
      <p class="eyebrow">UNDERSTAND THE WHOLE LITTLE SYSTEM</p>
      <h2>A light is the beginning of a bigger idea.</h2>
    </div>
    <div>
      <p>
        The circuit supplies energy. The microcontroller runs instructions. A
        signal tells each LED what colour to show. A network can carry a message
        from somewhere else. Following that chain connects electronics,
        computing and communications in one project you can see and investigate.
      </p>
      <p>
        LightVM is the lighting part of the project: write patterns using
        Python, JavaScript or visual blocks, preview them on screen, and run
        them on a small controller. The companion kaj-fi project explores sound
        analysis and sharing audio features between devices; its public firmware
        is not yet available.
      </p>
      <p>
        Play with an example, ask why it behaves that way, or make something of
        your own. You don’t need a board or an account to try the browser
        playground. Our learning path connects those first experiments to real
        circuits and, when you’re ready, connected devices.
      </p>
      <div class="actions">
        <a class="text-link" href={`${base}learn/`}>
          Choose a learning starting point →
        </a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="wrap section">
    <div class="section-heading">
      <p class="eyebrow">THREE THREADS, ONE PROJECT</p>
      <h2>
        Something to make.
        <br />
        Something to figure out.
      </h2>
    </div>
    <div class="subjects">
      <article>
        <span class="number">01</span>
        <h3>Electronics</h3>
        <p>
          Power, current, connections and signals. Trace the circuit, recognise
          the parts and learn why a data wire cannot power a strip of lights.
        </p>
        <a class="text-link" href={`${base}learn/#electronics`}>
          Follow the circuit →
        </a>
      </article>
      <article>
        <span class="number">02</span>
        <h3>Computing</h3>
        <p>
          Instructions, state, memory and time. Predict a pattern, change a rule
          and investigate how a processor turns a program into behaviour.
        </p>
        <a class="text-link" href={`${base}learn/#computing`}>
          Explore the instructions →
        </a>
      </article>
      <article>
        <span class="number">03</span>
        <h3>Networking & communications</h3>
        <p>
          Messages, addresses and shared rules. Explore how devices find one
          another, exchange information and behave when a connection goes away.
        </p>
        <a class="text-link" href={`${base}learn/#communications`}>
          Follow a message →
        </a>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="wrap section split" aria-labelledby="imagine-title">
    <div>
      <p class="eyebrow">MAKING IN AN AI-READY WORLD</p>
      <h2 id="imagine-title">
        Understanding gives imagination more to work with.
      </h2>
    </div>
    <div>
      <p>
        AI can help you write a program or develop an idea. Knowing what a
        sensor can measure, what a microcontroller can control and how a message
        travels helps you recognise possibilities, ask better questions and
        judge whether the result makes sense.
      </p>
      <p>
        “If you can’t imagine it, you can’t ask for it to be built.” The aim is
        to grow that imagination through experience: try something, explain what
        happened and use what you learn to make the next thing.
      </p>
      <a class="text-link" href={`${base}about/#why-understanding`}>
        Why this matters, and our link with Found in Space →
      </a>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="wrap section split" id="what-is-a-board">
    <div>
      <p class="eyebrow">FROM SCREEN TO REAL LIGHTS</p>
      <h2>What’s a board, anyway?</h2>
      <p>
        A board is a small circuit board with a programmable chip called a
        microcontroller, a USB socket and connection pins. It runs your lighting
        program and sends instructions to the LEDs. The LED strip is a separate
        part: the board is the controller, and the lights show the result.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div>
      <h3>Buy the board. Make the project.</h3>
      <p>
        You don’t need to design a circuit board from scratch. Start with an
        off-the-shelf Raspberry Pi Pico W or Pico 2 W, or one of the supported
        Seeed XIAO ESP32 models. You can buy these from electronics suppliers;
        our hardware guide links to the manufacturers and their retailers.
      </p>
      <p>
        “Build your own” means connecting that board to a few LEDs and
        installing LightVM, the software that runs on it. Choose the exact model
        in the guide and check firmware availability before buying: not every
        board with a similar name can use the same software.
      </p>
      <div class="actions">
        <a class="text-link" href={`${base}hardware/#find-a-board`}>
          Find a board →
        </a>
        <a class="text-link" href={`${base}build/#parts`}>
          See the starter parts list →
        </a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="setup-band">
    <div class="wrap split">
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow">BUILD YOUR OWN BOARD SETUP</p>
        <h2>
          Your first
          <br />
          little light project.
        </h2>
        <p>
          Start with a Pico, a USB data cable, an addressable LED strip or ring
          and connecting wires. USB powers the first build through the Pico’s
          VBUS pin. Make your first colour, try a challenge, then explore extra
          power and other upgrades as your project grows.
        </p>
      </div>
      <ol class="journey">
        <li>
          <span>1</span>
          <div>
            <h3>Gather the bits</h3>
            <p>
              Ask an adult helper to get a board ready and check the power.
              Fitted pins and wires make it easier to connect.
            </p>
          </div>
        </li>
        <li>
          <span>2</span>
          <div>
            <h3>Connect and set up</h3>
            <p>
              Follow the quick diagram: a Pico can send colour instructions
              straight to the first LED. Check the connections together.
            </p>
          </div>
        </li>
        <li>
          <span>3</span>
          <div>
            <h3>Make something glow</h3>
            <p>
              Choose a colour at low brightness. Invent a secret light signal,
              change a pattern, or investigate a mystery in the circuit.
            </p>
          </div>
        </li>
      </ol>
    </div>
    <div class="wrap actions">
      <a class="button dark" href={`${base}build/`}>
        Parts and build guide →
      </a>
      <a class="text-link" href={`${base}studio/#/devices`}>
        Already running KajLights? Open your devices →
      </a>
    </div>
  </section>
</Site>
